paragraphs:
An April ray was dancing on the china of the whatnot; the fire burned;
beneath her slippers she felt the softness of the carpet; the day was
bright, the air warm, and she heard her child shouting with laughter.

She succeeded at first in putting off Lheureux. At last he lost patience;
he was being sued; his capital was out, and unless he got some in he
should be forced to take back all the goods she had received.

And she was for leaving at once if the other did not apologise. So Charles
went back again to his wife and implored her to give way; he knelt to her;
she ended by saying—

It was like the sky when a gust of wind drives the clouds across. The mass
of sad thoughts that darkened them seemed to be lifted from her blue eyes;
her whole face shone. He waited. At last she replied—

He saw her alone in the evening, very late, behind the garden in the lane;
in the lane, as she had the other one! It was a stormy night, and they
talked under an umbrella by lightning flashes.

She wept; she even called him “her good Monsieur Lheureux.” But he always
fell back upon “that rascal Vincart.” Besides, he hadn’t a brass farthing;
no one was paying him now-a-days; they were eating his coat off his back;
a poor shopkeeper like him couldn’t advance money.

With the money she at last received from Barneville she paid two bills;
the other fifteen hundred francs fell due. She renewed the bills, and thus
it was continually.
